1
00:00:05,990 --> 00:01:00,010
 Some say it contains, this is about as many as there are stars in the Milky Way.

2
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:03,210
 Others say it probably has more than a hundred billion.

3
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:28,020
 To end, the neurons of the human brain equal the distance of the earth to the moon.

4
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:44,250
 The human brain developed approximately three billion years ago.

5
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:58,600
 There were algae, then bacteria, then invertebrates, then fish, reptiles.

6
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:17,200
 Two hundred million years ago, the primitive brain of the great reptile enabled them to survive through primitive responses alone.

7
00:02:18,810 --> 00:02:22,650
 For example, rest of defense of their territory.

8
00:02:29,610 --> 00:02:32,960
 Responses such as these also exist in modern man.

9
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:37,600
 Approximately a hundred million years ago, the paleocortex developed,

10
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:43,430
 incorporating emotions such as apprehension, fear, and the beginning of memory.

11
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:48,460
 In a situation of danger, the instinct for survival is demonstrated.

12
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:17,760
 Approximately a hundred thousand years ago, the neocortex developed.

13
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:22,340
 Represents 85% of the cerebral mass of the human brain.

14
00:03:22,890 --> 00:03:25,530
 Now the interpretation of ideas is made possible.

15
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:53,740
 Club equals food and hide and bones.

16
00:03:56,590 --> 00:04:02,740
 It is therefore the neocortex which enables man to think and reason, behave in a civilized manner.

17
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:14,780
 The reptilian brain, the paleocortex or primitive mammalian brain, and the neocortex or modern man's brain.

18
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:20,190
 Unfortunately, communication between them is not always perfect.

19
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:25,670
 Ah! Don't you look where you're going, you idiot!

20
00:04:26,130 --> 00:04:28,110
 You're not gonna let him get away with that, are you? Huh?

21
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:36,610
 Is there anything wrong here?

22
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:41,020
 Well then, move on.

23
00:04:44,150 --> 00:05:12,390
 Before we're approaching the brain, now you'll be able to see how this remarkable organ functions.

24
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:17,980
 It contains billions of neurons, with billions and billions of synaptic connections.

25
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:19,920
 You'll see it's extraordinary.

26
00:05:21,330 --> 00:05:25,260
 Possibly be of help, Professor, if the brain is such a perfect organ.

27
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:29,250
 Oxygen really so important to it?

28
00:05:29,390 --> 00:05:30,910
 Yes, very important, Globin.

29
00:05:30,910 --> 00:05:33,290
 The brain's needs are really enormous.

30
00:05:33,670 --> 00:05:38,330
 It uses 2,000 liters of blood a day, which provides 60 liters of air.

31
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:41,270
 This is 20% of the body's oxygen requirements.

32
00:05:41,610 --> 00:05:44,730
 Without us, if the brain were deprived of the oxygen we supply,

33
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:48,540
 fainting and then loss of consciousness would occur.

34
00:05:48,810 --> 00:05:51,480
 If the supply was cut off, say, a few minutes,

35
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:55,920
 the brain would suffer irreparable damage and the whole organism would simply die.

36
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:57,220
 Is anything wrong, Professor?

37
00:05:57,610 --> 00:06:00,180
 Have you lost your way? Oh, we'd better move.

38
00:06:00,530 --> 00:06:02,890
 Move along, move along. Please keep moving.

39
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:04,830
 It's not the place to stop and have a chat.

40
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:12,300
 There, gramps.

41
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:15,430
 Oh, heavens, what a day and age.

42
00:06:15,630 --> 00:06:17,190
 There's no respect for the elderly.

43
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:19,830
 My day, children were seen and not heard.

44
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:31,360
 You carry some of your oxygen bubbles, my pretty globin.

45
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:49,450
 Accelerate the blood circulation immediately.

46
00:06:49,830 --> 00:06:52,770
 I repeat, accelerate the blood circulation immediately.

47
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:55,920
 Please respond to an urgent request from the heart.

48
00:06:55,920 --> 00:07:04,340
 Oh, Professor, aren't you coming with us?

49
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,000
 Children, you see, we are independent of the rest of the body.

50
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:11,020
 What happens elsewhere doesn't concern us.

51
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:12,990
 If it has to do with the heart.

52
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:15,880
 Oh no, the brain doesn't obey, it commands.

53
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:20,240
 It has absolute priority, Globin, even over the heart.

54
00:07:21,980 --> 00:07:24,700
 Oh no, no, to you lot.

55
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:27,410
 That passage over there.

56
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:31,320
 But why are you coming with us?

57
00:07:31,890 --> 00:07:34,820
 Because oxygen is what the brain needs most.

58
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:37,010
 And also sugar.

59
00:07:38,190 --> 00:07:42,100
 But they have to take special passageways assigned to them.

60
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:54,430
 We'd be near enough to the neurons to give them oxygen.

61
00:07:55,330 --> 00:07:56,350
 Never, my dear.

62
00:07:57,480 --> 00:08:01,020
 Neurons are too sensitive to come into direct contact with us.

63
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,580
 It works rather differently, you'll see.

64
00:08:37,450 --> 00:08:52,450
 Jumbo, hurry!

65
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:08,970
 Inside the dendrite nerve end, a little messenger is waiting to relay the message along the axon.

66
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:25,240
 It's easy. Right, we'll transmit that message.

67
00:09:57,390 --> 00:10:31,180
 It's up into numerous nerve ends.

68
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:38,100
 Nerve cell has 100,000 synaptic connections.

69
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:41,420
 And as we know, the brain has 10 billion nerve cells.

70
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:46,690
 Imagine how many synaptic connections there are if these two figures are multiplied.

71
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:53,680
 Little chemical messengers now arrive at the nucleus of the neuron.

72
00:10:54,970 --> 00:10:58,250
 Pass on their messages to be processed.

73
00:11:01,880 --> 00:11:03,890
 So, what are they asking for?

74
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:05,870
 Um, Jumbo is thirsty, sir.

75
00:11:06,770 --> 00:11:08,690
 Terribly hot.

76
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:12,080
 Shall we ask for the breathing to be accelerated, sir, do you think?

77
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:13,560
 And also for a rising temperature, sir.

78
00:11:13,990 --> 00:11:17,420
 We'll send messages to the heart, the lungs and the diaphragm.

79
00:11:17,820 --> 00:11:19,720
 Isn't that right, sir?

80
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:21,570
 Have you finished showing off?

81
00:11:21,750 --> 00:11:25,920
 Your job is to transmit to the medulla oblongata, is that clear?

82
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:27,160
 It will decide the rest.

83
00:11:27,260 --> 00:11:28,300
 Get going now, all of you.

84
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:28,640
 Move.

85
00:11:41,990 --> 00:11:48,840
 Cortical zone R12.

86
00:11:49,230 --> 00:11:50,370
 Faster pulse rate.

87
00:11:50,570 --> 00:11:53,370
 Iron permeability insufficient in sector 2083.

88
00:11:53,670 --> 00:11:54,470
 Sending messengers.

89
00:11:54,590 --> 00:11:56,250
 He's thirsty, sir. What shall I do?

90
00:11:56,490 --> 00:11:59,390
 Transmit to the hypothalamus that it must order Jumbo to drink.

91
00:12:00,350 --> 00:12:03,640
 Don't waste time doing that. It's not that urgent.

92
00:12:04,250 --> 00:12:06,820
 Now look here. We've already made the temperature rise.

93
00:12:07,350 --> 00:12:09,190
 And also ask for perspiration.

94
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:12,310
 And the acceleration of the breathing.

95
00:12:12,810 --> 00:12:15,420
 But I still say we must transmit the order to drink.

96
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:17,900
 No, we must first ask the heart to beat faster.

97
00:12:18,420 --> 00:12:20,960
 And the lungs to breathe harder and the thermoregulation center...

98
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:22,180
 No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

99
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:24,060
 We cannot ask for everything all at once.

100
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:25,920
 We must get our priorities right.

101
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:27,220
 Oh, you old fool.

102
00:12:27,460 --> 00:12:28,500
 You're the old fool.

103
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:29,700
 That idiot.

104
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:31,080
 Now wait, huh?

105
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:35,270
 We'll transmit everything to the forebrain and it will make the right decisions.

106
00:12:36,260 --> 00:12:37,310
 Brother, good lord.

107
00:12:38,290 --> 00:12:40,290
 What about our responsibility?

108
00:12:55,010 --> 00:13:19,830
 It's really at least 1,000 times faster than we do.

109
00:13:20,620 --> 00:13:23,400
 Yes, but that's only the transmission of the orders.

110
00:13:23,580 --> 00:13:28,410
 Each order relies on previous knowledge, memory, experience, and reason.

111
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:31,440
 How does the brain manage to know so much?

112
00:13:31,770 --> 00:13:34,290
 That globin is the great mystery of the brain.

113
00:13:34,710 --> 00:13:38,810
 Quantity of data that it is capable of storing defies imagination.

114
00:13:39,310 --> 00:13:41,100
 Please tell us more, Professor.

115
00:13:41,630 --> 00:13:44,130
 Hmm, well, let me see. Hmm.

116
00:13:44,890 --> 00:13:47,130
 Move along there, please. Hurry up.

117
00:13:47,450 --> 00:13:53,370
 Well, well, the brain receives information from all the senses and all the organs as well.

118
00:13:53,370 --> 00:13:58,570
 It then distributes the information among its neurons, or nerve cells, if you prefer.

119
00:13:59,010 --> 00:14:02,150
 It's thought to happen more or less as follows.

120
00:14:14,090 --> 00:14:57,750
 We must register that a thorn pricks.

121
00:15:01,050 --> 00:15:07,840
 Register that the prick of a thorn hurts.

122
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:22,500
 A thorn prick causes a wound that bleeds.

123
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:28,660
 Please commence manufacture of proteins for the storage of this information.

124
00:15:32,550 --> 00:15:33,690
 Now, where are those proteins?

125
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:38,030
 We must hurry. The information to be stored is arriving.

126
00:16:08,560 --> 00:16:34,270
 This way for the pain, please. Straight ahead for the blood.

127
00:16:34,270 --> 00:16:36,220
 Blood is red, this way please.

128
00:16:36,220 --> 00:16:37,920
 Blood is sticky, this way please.

129
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:39,320
 Blood stains, this way please.

130
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:40,920
 Blood is warm, this way please.

131
00:16:40,920 --> 00:17:08,780
 Hot, hot, quite simple.

132
00:17:08,780 --> 00:17:11,720
 No, Globin is not that simple at all.

133
00:17:11,720 --> 00:17:16,210
 Because when a thorn pricks you, it isn't just the few messengers I mentioned to you

134
00:17:16,210 --> 00:17:19,330
 that carry the necessary information to the brain for storage.

135
00:17:19,330 --> 00:17:27,780
 Thousands upon thousands, each one carries a different message.

136
00:17:27,780 --> 00:17:31,780
 Information about this drop of blood will be filed under a great many headings,

137
00:17:31,780 --> 00:17:34,780
 such as smell, taste, temperature and composition.

138
00:17:34,780 --> 00:18:30,460
 Register that it smells good.

139
00:18:30,460 --> 00:18:39,550
 Register that a rose is soft.

140
00:18:39,550 --> 00:18:40,960
 Mmm.

141
00:18:40,960 --> 00:18:50,750
 Register that a rose is beautiful, sir.

142
00:18:50,750 --> 00:19:03,230
 Register that a rose is as light as a feather.

143
00:19:03,230 --> 00:19:07,990
 Register that a rose is fragile.

144
00:19:07,990 --> 00:19:14,720
 Thorns can hurt. Register that.

145
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:26,590
 Rose is pink.

146
00:19:26,590 --> 00:19:27,290
 No!

147
00:19:27,290 --> 00:19:27,790
 What?

148
00:19:27,790 --> 00:19:28,320
 Red!

149
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:29,120
 A rose is pink!

150
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:29,920
 A rose is white!

151
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:31,120
 No, a rose is yellow!

152
00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:32,620
 No, I'm telling you, a rose is pink!

153
00:19:32,620 --> 00:19:36,350
 Stop! Stop! That's enough now! Stop arguing! In fact, you're all correct.

154
00:19:36,350 --> 00:19:39,800
 Because roses can be all those colours and more.

155
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:43,900
 Colours she saw must be registered, so get a move on.

156
00:19:43,900 --> 00:19:49,470
 But, Professor, how does the brain manage to store all that information and what happens to it?

157
00:19:49,470 --> 00:19:52,710
 Oh dear, you have not been paying attention, young Globus.

158
00:19:52,710 --> 00:19:57,850
 When, for example, the eye receives information or perceives danger,

159
00:19:57,850 --> 00:20:02,130
 it sends this message to the brain, which draws on its stored knowledge

160
00:20:02,130 --> 00:20:15,540
 and orders the necessary action to be taken.

161
00:20:15,540 --> 00:20:43,410
 Transmit that she must be very careful.

162
00:20:43,410 --> 00:21:29,140
 Thorns prick. There's a risk he might lose his balance.

163
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:31,770
 Oh, I don't think we need to worry about that, sir.

164
00:21:31,770 --> 00:21:35,010
 Surely he will react by reflex straight from the spinal cord.

165
00:21:35,010 --> 00:21:36,860
 Let's not take any chances, shall we?

166
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:38,580
 Transmit to the regions concerned.

167
00:21:45,580 --> 00:21:52,670
 Bellum control room.

168
00:21:52,670 --> 00:21:54,880
 There's a risk of loss of balance.

169
00:21:54,880 --> 00:21:57,120
 Automatic regulation through reflex action.

170
00:21:57,120 --> 00:21:59,170
 Activate automatic regulation.

171
00:21:59,170 --> 00:22:01,930
 Inform the cortex to take emergency measures.

172
00:22:01,930 --> 00:22:03,930
 This is the cortex control room.

173
00:22:03,930 --> 00:22:08,290
 Order left leg to move forward quickly to help regain balance.

174
00:22:08,290 --> 00:22:13,090
 Instruct the hypothalamus to order the adrenal glands to pump adrenaline.

175
00:22:13,090 --> 00:22:22,140
 As a precaution, bring the arms forward in order to absorb the shock if he should fall.

176
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:39,070
 Knows mainly those things we consciously inform it of.

177
00:22:39,070 --> 00:22:43,820
 For the rest, it stores only about one hundredth of what we unconsciously perceive,

178
00:22:44,330 --> 00:22:47,590
 because the brain receives all the information at the same time.

179
00:22:48,210 --> 00:22:52,500
 Noise, shape, colour, touch, feelings, association of ideas.

180
00:22:53,610 --> 00:22:56,710
 It stores the most important information.

181
00:22:57,110 --> 00:22:59,300
 But which part of the brain gives orders, Professor?

182
00:22:59,690 --> 00:23:03,790
 Well, each part of the body is controlled by a specific area of the brain.

183
00:23:04,130 --> 00:23:08,110
 A larger area is required by the more sensitive parts of the body.

184
00:23:08,110 --> 00:23:10,570
 For example, the face and the hands.

185
00:23:14,440 --> 00:23:17,240
 Tell me, sir, does the brain have total control of the individual?

186
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:20,410
 Oh, well, that's more or less right.

187
00:23:20,510 --> 00:23:24,150
 But the communication between the different parts of the brain isn't always perfect.

188
00:23:24,510 --> 00:23:27,170
 You see, the most primitive part always tries to dominate.

189
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:34,890
 These two out.

190
00:23:35,210 --> 00:23:36,930
 Just listen to that healthy cough.

191
00:23:37,790 --> 00:23:48,630
 Pulse, increase the blood pressure, make more sugar, get ready to attack.

192
00:23:53,180 --> 00:24:01,850
 It's all worked up about such a trivial matter.

193
00:24:02,050 --> 00:24:04,390
 Slow the heart down, get rid of the extra sugar.

194
00:24:04,820 --> 00:24:06,430
 Why doesn't he mind his own business?

195
00:24:07,450 --> 00:24:10,680
 Control here!

196
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:27,720
 Brain contains a hundred thousand million cells.

197
00:24:28,750 --> 00:24:31,350
 Billion, billion, billion, billion, billion connections.

198
00:24:31,940 --> 00:24:34,420
 It is capable of a million reactions per minute.

199
00:24:35,150 --> 00:24:39,710
 To write the number of possible connections, you'd need a figure with ten million kilometers of noughts.

200
00:24:40,220 --> 00:24:43,620
 Or 26 times the distance between the Earth and the Moon.

201
00:24:43,620 --> 00:24:48,440
 And that ends our story of the brain, the most fascinating organ in the universe.

